#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167895} :activity/id #uuid "9f3fa68b-4b95-5afb-803a-30726de37cbc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049381 :topic/id #uuid "f0242aed-46fd-5f2e-bcc2-49ee070cdccf" :topic/math-academy-id 3190} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167993 :tutorial/content "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 1**. In the initial value problem above, we apply the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation.\n\nFirst, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{'}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+5} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{'}(t)}=L{-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+5L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{x}_{2}(t)}-2L{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the result\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively.\n\n**Step 2**. Now, solving the first equation for $X_1(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{5}{s}\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2s+5}{s(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns X_2(s) - 3 = - 2 \\left(\\dfrac{-2s+5}{s(s-1)}\\right) + 4 X_2(s) - \\dfrac{2}{s}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\dfrac{3s^2-s-8}{s(s-1)(s-4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**. To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{-2s+5}{s(s-1)}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{s-1}-\\frac{5}{s}} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3{s}^{2}-s-8}{s(s-1)(s-4)}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s-1}+\\frac{3}{s-4}-\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}+3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-4}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}+3{e}^{4t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t}-5 \\\\ 2{e}^{t}+3{e}^{4t}-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "00904ad0-6191-560b-a452-c838aa7543a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14420 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "822d22ea-2253-5461-b518-1ae1ae238fee" :step/math-academy-id 590705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166530 :question/id #uuid "008364d3-24bd-58e2-a8ab-f0d4d881e918" :question/math-academy-id "e-21575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca890a8e-9ddd-51f7-9784-c9c1f020adef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391080}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "3d8f9646-10b3-5bfd-97ef-e080e3440cfc" :step/math-academy-id 590706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166531 :question/id #uuid "230e2550-b584-53fd-9da4-4128584124dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21576"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b22e449-2966-59cf-b012-3f5721b4bc98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308371}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Laplace Transformed Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "6351ca80-d1fa-5039-94d0-87986551c3b2" :step/math-academy-id 590709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166532 :question/id #uuid "748ef205-542b-5763-9cdd-00dc9ff1d181" :question/math-academy-id "e-21577"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff314a2-9059-5a37-8c2c-2b7a2fe6dcd9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050768}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385173}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Laplace Transformed Solution"} :step/id #uuid "5d6348a1-6da4-5516-b665-5319df7cd619" :step/math-academy-id 590712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168032 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to use Laplace transforms to solve inhomogeneous systems like the one below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilar to the homogeneous case, the process can be split into three steps.\n\n- **Step 1.** Apply the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation, to get a system with variables \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Solve the obtained system for variables \n\n$$\nX_1(s) \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad X_2(s).\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** To find initial variables \n\n$$\nx_1(t) \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad x_2(t)\n$$\n\nsatisfying the original initial value problem, apply the inverse Laplace transform to \n\n$$\nX_1(s) \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad X_2(s).\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "3733acd7-1a13-5183-a501-0baf1eaf53a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14611 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91a3017f-8462-5088-8b1f-303f10ce818d" :step/math-academy-id 590715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167891}}] :activity/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166988}]}
